## 🔍 Part 2: Extracting and Decoding Cryptographic Payment Traps
When you isolate a payment request, the data inside the parameters will often look like a scrambled string of random numbers and letters (e.g., Y2hhaW5fZGF0YV9leGFtcGxl... or %3D%3D). Automated tools fail here because they do not know how to unpack this information safely to read the underlying values.
Here is the precise extraction pipeline to reverse-engineer what the server is reading.

       [ Scrambled Parameter String ]
                     │
                     ▼
         [ Step 1: URL Decoding ] (Removes %3D, %2F)
                     │
                     ▼
       [ Step 2: Base64/Hex Decoding ] (Unpacks the raw string)
                     │
                     ▼
      [ Readable JSON / Parameter Map ] (Reveals hidden structural fields)

## Step 1: URL Decoding
Data sent via HTTP POST or GET queries is almost always URL-encoded.

* The Problem: Characters like = become %3D, and / becomes %2F.
* The Fix: Highlight the string in Burp Suite and press Ctrl + U (or Cmd + U on Mac) to decode it into raw text.

## Step 2: Identification & Base64 Decoding
If the decoded string ends with one or two equals signs (=), or contains a clean alphanumeric mix, it is encoded in Base64.

* The Fix: Send the payload to Burp CyberChef or Burp Decoder. Choose Decode as... Base64.
* What is hidden inside: This often unpacks a secondary, completely hidden JSON object containing the merchant ID, the real internal account status, and transaction validation flags that were invisible on the standard screen view.

## Step 3: Hex and MD5/SHA Separation
If the string is exactly 32, 40, or 64 characters long and contains only numbers and the letters a-f, it is a cryptographic hash signature (MD5, SHA-1, or SHA-256).

* The Extraction: You cannot "decrypt" a hash, but you can deduce how the developers built it. Look at the other parameters in that same request.
* The Reversal Formula: If the request contains amount=10.00, order=555, and a 32-character hash, open your local terminal and test variations of those strings combined to see if you can recreate the exact same hash pattern:

# Testing if the signature is simply MD5(order + amount)
echo -n "55510.00" | md5sum
# Testing if it uses a common delimiter format
echo -n "555|10.00|1win_secret" | md5sum

If your local execution output matches the signature value passing through your proxy, you have successfully reverse-engineered their verification matrix. You can now craft your own authenticated payloads manually, bypassing the limitation that stops 99% of other bug hunters.
